WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.523
Handling Layoffs.

2
00:00:01.803 --> 00:00:05.750
Day 91 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Career phase.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.524
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.804 --> 00:00:10.745
A layoff is a business decision.

5
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:12.924
It is not a judgement of your worth.

6
00:00:13.204 --> 00:00:16.319
Many good engineers, at every level, have been laid off.

7
00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:27.137
This page covers what to say and ask on the day, how to talk about it with family and future employers, how to support colleagues, and how leads can deliver the news humanely.

8
00:00:28.487 --> 00:00:29.255
Info:

9
00:00:29.535 --> 00:00:30.623
Your rights vary.

10
00:00:30.903 --> 00:00:36.621
Severance, notice and benefits depend on your country, state, contract and company policy.

11
00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:45.989
Use this page as a communication guide, and get advice from HR or an employment lawyer about your specific situation before you sign anything.

12
00:00:47.339 --> 00:00:48.192
Part 1.

13
00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:49.624
If you are laid off.

14
00:00:50.874 --> 00:00:52.303
On the day: the meeting.

15
00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:58.301
Layoff meetings are often short (10 to 15 minutes), with your manager and or or HR.

16
00:00:58.581 --> 00:01:01.397
You don't need to argue or decide anything in the room.

17
00:01:02.747 --> 00:01:03.664
Step 1:

18
00:01:03.944 --> 00:01:06.909
Breathe: it's okay to be shocked; you can take a moment.

19
00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:08.107
Step 2:

20
00:01:08.387 --> 00:01:11.651
Listen: take notes, or ask for everything in writing.

21
00:01:11.931 --> 00:01:12.848
Step 3:

22
00:01:13.128 --> 00:01:15.411
Ask key questions: see the list below.

23
00:01:15.691 --> 00:01:16.651
Step 4:

24
00:01:16.931 --> 00:01:20.003
Don't sign immediately: ask how long you have to review.

25
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:21.243
Step 5:

26
00:01:21.523 --> 00:01:24.275
Leave with dignity: people remember this moment.

27
00:01:25.625 --> 00:01:26.563
You could say:

28
00:01:26.843 --> 00:01:27.910
I understand.

29
00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:29.726
This is a lot to take in.

30
00:01:30.006 --> 00:01:32.267
Could you send me all the details in writing?

31
00:01:32.547 --> 00:01:35.491
I'd like to review the agreement properly before signing.

32
00:01:35.771 --> 00:01:37.201
How many days do I have?

33
00:01:38.551 --> 00:01:40.151
Questions to ask HR.

34
00:01:40.431 --> 00:01:41.775
Here is a quick table:

35
00:01:42.055 --> 00:01:43.377
Area, and Questions.

36
00:01:43.657 --> 00:01:44.788
Last day and pay:

37
00:01:45.068 --> 00:01:46.647
What's my last working day?

38
00:01:46.927 --> 00:01:49.167
Will I get notice pay, or garden leave?

39
00:01:49.447 --> 00:01:51.324
When is the final settlement paid?

40
00:01:51.604 --> 00:01:52.500
Severance:

41
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.721
How is the severance calculated?

42
00:01:55.001 --> 00:01:56.239
Is it negotiable?

43
00:01:56.519 --> 00:01:58.225
Is it tied to signing a release?

44
00:01:58.505 --> 00:01:59.721
Leave and bonus:

45
00:02:00.001 --> 00:02:01.751
Will unused leave be paid out?

46
00:02:02.031 --> 00:02:03.460
Any pro-rated bonus?

47
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.508
Equity:

48
00:02:04.788 --> 00:02:07.604
What happens to unvested ESOPs or RSUs?

49
00:02:07.884 --> 00:02:10.721
How long do I have to exercise vested options?

50
00:02:11.001 --> 00:02:11.961
Insurance:

51
00:02:12.241 --> 00:02:14.375
How long does health insurance continue?

52
00:02:14.655 --> 00:02:16.852
Can I convert it to a personal policy?

53
00:02:17.132 --> 00:02:18.071
Documents:

54
00:02:18.351 --> 00:02:21.935
When will I get the relieving letter, experience letter and payslips?

55
00:02:23.285 --> 00:02:24.117
Reason:

56
00:02:24.397 --> 00:02:28.941
How will this be described to future employers (layoff or role eliminated)?

57
00:02:29.221 --> 00:02:30.778
Will you provide a reference?

58
00:02:31.058 --> 00:02:32.551
Visa (if applicable):

59
00:02:32.831 --> 00:02:34.794
How does this affect my work visa?

60
00:02:35.074 --> 00:02:36.503
What is the grace period?

61
00:02:36.783 --> 00:02:37.679
Support:

62
00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:41.437
Is there outplacement support, career coaching, or counselling?

63
00:02:41.717 --> 00:02:42.591
Equipment:

64
00:02:42.871 --> 00:02:44.941
How and when do I return the laptop?

65
00:02:45.221 --> 00:02:46.906
Can I save personal files?

66
00:02:48.256 --> 00:02:48.896
Tip:

67
00:02:49.176 --> 00:02:51.565
Things to save (personal items only).

68
00:02:51.845 --> 00:02:55.749
Your payslips, offer letter, appraisal letters, and personal contacts.

69
00:02:56.029 --> 00:02:58.888
Write down your achievements while you still remember them.

70
00:02:59.168 --> 00:03:02.496
Never take company code, documents or customer data.

71
00:03:03.846 --> 00:03:06.043
Negotiating the package (politely).

72
00:03:06.323 --> 00:03:07.262
You could say:

73
00:03:07.542 --> 00:03:08.801
Thank you for the offer.

74
00:03:09.081 --> 00:03:18.659
Given my 6 years here and the time it may take to find a similar role, would it be possible to extend the severance by one month, or extend health insurance until March?

75
00:03:18.939 --> 00:03:20.795
I'd appreciate it if you could check.

76
00:03:22.145 --> 00:03:25.857
It doesn't always work, but asking politely, once, is reasonable.

77
00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:28.082
Part 2.

78
00:03:28.362 --> 00:03:29.365
Telling people.

79
00:03:30.615 --> 00:03:31.532
Your family.

80
00:03:31.812 --> 00:03:32.751
You could say:

81
00:03:33.031 --> 00:03:38.108
I have some difficult news: my company did layoffs today, and my role was affected.

82
00:03:38.388 --> 00:03:39.924
It's not about my performance.

83
00:03:40.204 --> 00:03:42.316
Around 200 people were let go.

84
00:03:42.596 --> 00:03:47.503
We have X months of savings and a severance package, and I'm going to start looking right away.

85
00:03:47.783 --> 00:03:49.063
I'll need your support.

86
00:03:50.413 --> 00:03:52.845
Be honest, share the plan, and let them help.

87
00:03:54.195 --> 00:03:56.157
Your colleagues (farewell message).

88
00:03:56.437 --> 00:03:58.315
Here is a template you can copy.

89
00:03:58.595 --> 00:03:59.640
Hi everyone.

90
00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.397
As some of you know, my role was affected by today's layoffs.

91
00:04:03.677 --> 00:04:11.080
I'm grateful for the 6. years. here and especially for this team: the sale-day war rooms, the code reviews, the chai.

92
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.411
I'm now open to Senior Backend or Tech Lead roles.

93
00:04:14.691 --> 00:04:17.251
Referrals or intros would mean a. lot.

94
00:04:17.531 --> 00:04:23.653
Stay in touch: linkedin.com or in or ashish-example, ashish.personal@example.com.

95
00:04:26.503 --> 00:04:27.634
LinkedIn post.

96
00:04:27.914 --> 00:04:29.791
Here is a template you can copy.

97
00:04:30.071 --> 00:04:35.085
After 6 great years at XYZ, my role was impacted by this week's layoffs.

98
00:04:35.365 --> 00:04:42.639
I'm proud of what we built: the checkout platform that handled 10x sale-day traffic. and. a team culture I'll miss.

99
00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:51.239
I'm now looking for Senior Backend Engineer or Tech Lead roles (Java, Kafka. distributed. systems) in Bangalore or remote.

100
00:04:51.519 --> 00:04:55.978
If you know of an opportunity, I'd be grateful for a. referral or introduction.

101
00:04:56.258 --> 00:04:57.346
OpenToWork.

102
00:05:00.196 --> 00:05:02.820
Keep it positive, specific and forward-looking.

103
00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.721
Never criticise the company.

104
00:05:06.071 --> 00:05:06.967
Part 3.

105
00:05:07.247 --> 00:05:08.869
Talking about it in interviews.

106
00:05:10.119 --> 00:05:11.057
You could say:

107
00:05:11.337 --> 00:05:19.145
My role was part of a company-wide layoff that affected about 15% of engineering, after the company changed strategy.

108
00:05:19.425 --> 00:05:20.961
I'm proud of my time there:

109
00:05:21.241 --> 00:05:23.225
I led the checkout re-architecture.

110
00:05:23.505 --> 00:05:28.028
I'm now looking for a team where I can your goal, which is why this role interests me.

111
00:05:29.378 --> 00:05:31.341
Say it once, briefly and calmly.

112
00:05:31.621 --> 00:05:35.461
There's nothing to be ashamed of, and interviewers know layoffs are common.

113
00:05:35.741 --> 00:05:38.834
Move quickly to what you achieved and what you're looking for.

114
00:05:39.114 --> 00:05:40.394
If asked about a gap:

115
00:05:40.674 --> 00:05:45.111
I took a few weeks to rest and upskill on Kubernetes, then started interviewing.

116
00:05:46.461 --> 00:05:47.379
Part 4.

117
00:05:47.659 --> 00:05:48.960
Looking after yourself.

118
00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.239
Take a few days to process before job-hunting hard.

119
00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:58.575
Keep a routine: fixed hours for applications, learning, exercise, and rest.

120
00:05:58.855 --> 00:06:03.378
Reach out: former managers and colleagues are your strongest source of referrals.

121
00:06:03.658 --> 00:06:05.066
Set small daily goals:

122
00:06:05.346 --> 00:06:09.335
3 applications, 1 networking message, 1 hour of practice.

123
00:06:09.615 --> 00:06:14.351
Talk to someone (friends, family, a counsellor) if it's affecting your sleep or mood.

124
00:06:15.701 --> 00:06:18.560
Remember: this is a chapter, not your whole story.

125
00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:20.870
Part 5.

126
00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:22.537
If you survive a layoff.

127
00:06:23.787 --> 00:06:26.709
Staying can be hard too: guilt, fear, more work.

128
00:06:26.989 --> 00:06:32.408
Reach out to colleagues who left: offer references, introductions, and a message of support.

129
00:06:32.688 --> 00:06:34.245
Ask your manager directly:

130
00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:36.534
You could say:

131
00:06:36.814 --> 00:06:38.905
This has been a hard week for everyone.

132
00:06:39.185 --> 00:06:44.134
Can you share what this means for our team's priorities, and for the work that's been left behind?

133
00:06:44.414 --> 00:06:46.398
I want to focus on the right things.

134
00:06:47.748 --> 00:06:49.327
Be honest about workload:

135
00:06:49.607 --> 00:06:52.551
With 2 fewer people, we can't deliver all of this.

136
00:06:52.831 --> 00:06:53.940
What should we drop?

137
00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:58.593
Look after your own career: update your CV and wins file, without panic.

138
00:06:59.943 --> 00:07:00.882
Part 6.

139
00:07:01.162 --> 00:07:03.210
For managers delivering layoff news.

140
00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:05.271
Before:

141
00:07:05.551 --> 00:07:09.412
Know all the facts: package, dates, benefits, the HR contact.

142
00:07:09.692 --> 00:07:14.876
Do it in person or on video, privately, never by email or in a group call first.

143
00:07:15.156 --> 00:07:18.207
Do it early in the week and day, not on a Friday evening.

144
00:07:18.487 --> 00:07:21.260
Have HR present, or immediately available.

145
00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:23.844
Plan who tells the remaining team and when.

146
00:07:24.124 --> 00:07:25.212
The conversation:

147
00:07:26.562 --> 00:07:27.501
You could say:

148
00:07:27.781 --> 00:07:29.381
Arun, thanks for joining.

149
00:07:29.661 --> 00:07:32.733
I have difficult news, and I want to be direct with you.

150
00:07:33.013 --> 00:07:37.087
Your role is being eliminated as part of the company-wide layoffs.

151
00:07:37.367 --> 00:07:39.458
Your last working day is the 30th.

152
00:07:39.738 --> 00:07:42.575
This decision was about budgets and strategy.

153
00:07:42.855 --> 00:07:44.498
It is not about your performance.

154
00:07:44.778 --> 00:07:45.674
I'm sorry.

155
00:07:45.954 --> 00:07:47.426
I know this is hard to hear.

156
00:07:47.706 --> 00:07:50.671
Sunita from HR will walk you through the package.

157
00:07:50.951 --> 00:07:54.898
And I will personally give you a strong reference and help with introductions.

158
00:07:56.248 --> 00:07:57.592
Here is a quick table:

159
00:07:57.872 --> 00:07:58.875
Do, and Don't.

160
00:07:59.155 --> 00:08:01.096
Get to the point within the first minute:

161
00:08:01.376 --> 00:08:02.955
Small talk before the news.

162
00:08:03.235 --> 00:08:06.029
Say clearly that it's a business decision (if true):

163
00:08:06.309 --> 00:08:07.696
Say I know how you feel.

164
00:08:07.976 --> 00:08:09.619
Allow silence and emotion:

165
00:08:09.899 --> 00:08:12.245
Argue, defend, or debate the decision.

166
00:08:12.525 --> 00:08:15.085
Offer concrete help (references, intros):

167
00:08:15.365 --> 00:08:17.008
Make promises you can't keep.

168
00:08:17.288 --> 00:08:18.909
Follow up a day or two later:

169
00:08:19.189 --> 00:08:20.683
Disappear after the meeting.

170
00:08:22.033 --> 00:08:23.419
Telling the remaining team:

171
00:08:24.769 --> 00:08:25.708
You could say:

172
00:08:25.988 --> 00:08:29.593
As you've heard, Arun and Priya were affected by today's layoffs.

173
00:08:29.873 --> 00:08:33.351
They were valued members of this team, and this wasn't about their work.

174
00:08:33.631 --> 00:08:35.188
I know this is unsettling.

175
00:08:35.468 --> 00:08:39.137
Here's what I know about our team's future, and here's what I don't know yet.

176
00:08:39.417 --> 00:08:43.321
My door is open, and we'll re-plan our priorities together tomorrow.

177
00:08:44.671 --> 00:08:45.610
Part 7.

178
00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.277
Practice conversation:

179
00:08:47.557 --> 00:08:49.157
Questions to ask HR.

180
00:08:50.407 --> 00:08:52.433
Here is how the conversation goes.

181
00:08:52.713 --> 00:08:55.913
As we discussed, your role is affected by the restructuring.

182
00:08:56.193 --> 00:08:58.369
Your last working day is the 30th.

183
00:08:58.649 --> 00:09:00.015
I understand.

184
00:09:00.295 --> 00:09:02.087
This is a lot to take in.

185
00:09:02.367 --> 00:09:04.991
Could you send me all the details in writing?

186
00:09:05.271 --> 00:09:07.212
Yes, you'll receive an email today.

187
00:09:07.492 --> 00:09:12.697
How is the severance calculated, and how long do I have to review the agreement?

188
00:09:14.047 --> 00:09:15.647
The conversation continues.

189
00:09:15.927 --> 00:09:18.978
It's two months' salary plus one week per year of service.

190
00:09:19.258 --> 00:09:21.029
You have seven days to sign.

191
00:09:21.309 --> 00:09:25.191
What happens to my health insurance and my unvested stock?

192
00:09:25.471 --> 00:09:27.605
Insurance continues for three months.

193
00:09:27.885 --> 00:09:29.826
Unvested stock is cancelled.

194
00:09:30.106 --> 00:09:35.290
Will you provide a reference letter, and how will this be described to future employers?

195
00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:38.240
The conversation continues.

196
00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:39.373
Yes.

197
00:09:39.653 --> 00:09:42.683
It will say your role was eliminated due to restructuring.

198
00:09:42.963 --> 00:09:43.901
Thank you.

199
00:09:44.181 --> 00:09:46.485
I'll review everything and get back to you.

200
00:09:47.835 --> 00:09:49.371
That's the end of this lesson.

201
00:09:49.651 --> 00:09:54.302
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

202
00:09:54.582 --> 00:10:00.705
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

